DEEP[25]=('These are Kṛṣṇa’s only words in chapter 1, five words in all: <i>pārtha paśyaitān samavetān kurūn iti</i>. His next speech begins at 2.2. The great teaching is about to begin from a single command: look.',
 '<i>Paśya</i>, “behold”, is a word the Gītā will use again at its high point: <i>paśya me pārtha rūpāṇi</i>, “behold my forms, Pārtha” (11.5). The first “behold” shows Arjuna his family and breaks him; the second shows him the cosmic form and remakes him. Commentators note too that Kṛṣṇa says <i>samavetān</i>, “gathered”, the very word Dhṛtarāṣṭra used in 1.1. Some Vaiṣṇava teachers read Kṛṣṇa’s choice of spot as compassion: he lets Arjuna’s hidden attachment show, because a hidden illness cannot be treated. Others say simply that Kṛṣṇa did as he was told, and Arjuna’s eyes did the rest.',
 'Sometimes the most useful thing a friend can say is just “look”. Not advice, not judgment: an invitation to see what is in front of us.')
DEEP[26]=('<i>Pitṝn</i>, “fathers”, includes Bhūriśravas, of his father’s generation. <i>Mātulān</i>, maternal uncles, includes Śalya, brother of Mādrī, who fought for the Kauravas after Duryodhana tricked him with hospitality. <i>Sakhīn</i>, friends, includes old companions from the court of Hastināpura.',
 'The list is built of plain family words: fathers, grandfathers, teachers, uncles, brothers, sons, grandsons, friends. Sañjaya keeps it close to how a child would name relatives, not how a general would name units. Commentators note that Arjuna was not ignorant of who would be there; he had known for months. Knowing is one thing; seeing is another. The difference between information and realisation runs through the whole Gītā. Kṛṣṇa will later distinguish <i>jñāna</i>, knowledge, from <i>vijñāna</i>, realised knowledge (7.2, 9.1). Arjuna’s crisis begins when a known fact becomes a seen truth.',
 'We can know something for years and still be shaken when we finally see it. Give yourself grace when that happens. It is the mind catching up with the heart.')
DEEP[27]=('<i>Śvaśurān</i>, fathers-in-law: Drupada, Draupadī’s father, is on Arjuna’s side; but other in-laws, through his many marriages and alliances, were on the other. <i>Suhṛdaḥ</i>, well-wishers, are friends who wish one well without any tie of blood.',
 '<i>Senayor ubhayor api</i>, “in both armies too”: this is the key phrase. Arjuna’s grief is not just for the enemy but for everyone. He does not see a good army and a bad army any more; he sees a family about to destroy itself. Some commentators find this a sign of Arjuna’s fine nature: he is not a man who can kill without feeling. Kṛṣṇa will not try to make him feel less. He will teach him to act rightly without losing the feeling, through equanimity (2.48) and without hatred (11.55, 12.13).',
 'Feeling deeply for others is not something to cure. The aim is to act well while still feeling, without being swept away.')
DEEP[28]=('<i>Kṛpayā parayāviṣṭaḥ</i>, “possessed by great pity”: the verb <i>āviṣṭa</i> is used for a person seized or possessed. Arjuna is not choosing pity; it has taken hold of him. Verse 28 begins Arjuna’s long speech, which runs to the end of the chapter.',
 'The Gītā is precise about states of mind. Arjuna is <i>viṣīdan</i>, sinking. Later Kṛṣṇa calls this state <i>kaśmalam</i>, faint-heartedness (2.2), and <i>kṣudraṃ hṛdaya-daurbalyam</i>, petty weakness of heart (2.3). Strong words for a good man’s grief. Commentators explain that Kṛṣṇa is not condemning compassion. He condemns the confusion that turns a duty into a private sorrow. Rāmānuja says Arjuna’s pity was mixed with mistaken ideas of the Self; Śaṅkara says grief and delusion (<i>śoka</i> and <i>moha</i>) are the root problem the whole Gītā sets out to remove.',
 'Notice when a feeling “takes over” rather than being felt. That is the moment to pause and breathe, before any big decision.')
DEEP[29]=('',
 'The phrase <i>roma-harṣa</i>, hair standing on end, returns at the end of the Gītā. There Sañjaya calls the dialogue <i>roma-harṣaṇam</i>, “hair-raising” (18.74), and Arjuna at the cosmic vision is <i>hṛṣṭa-romā</i> (11.14). The same bodily sign marks fear at the start and wonder at the end. The book moves Arjuna from one kind of shiver to another. Modern readers have recognised in these verses an accurate description of an acute stress reaction. The tradition does not label it so, but it treats the body as a mirror of the mind and teaches that steadying the mind (chapter 6) steadies the body too.',
 'Your body’s signals are information, not failure. Breathe, slow down, and let the storm pass before you decide anything.')
DEEP[30]=('Gāṇḍīva could not be broken, and it never left Arjuna’s grip in battle. Until now.',
 'Commentators see a progression: from body (limbs, mouth, skin) to mind (reeling). Later Kṛṣṇa will describe the one of steady wisdom as <i>sthita-prajña</i> (2.54–72): the one who stands. The whole of chapter 2 can be read as the answer to “I cannot stand”. The Gītā’s word for steadiness, <i>sthiti</i>, runs from here to Arjuna’s last words: <i>sthito’smi</i>, “I stand firm” (18.73).',
 'When everything we rely on slips from our hands, we have a chance to find what does not slip.')
DEEP[31]=('Omens, <i>nimittāni</i>, were taken seriously in the epic. Before the war, the Mahābhārata describes terrible signs: comets, eclipses, jackals howling, blood-rain. Vyāsa describes them to Dhṛtarāṣṭra in the Bhīṣma Parva just before the Gītā.',
 'Arjuna calls Kṛṣṇa <i>Keśava</i>, “the one with beautiful hair”, or in another reading, slayer of the demon Keśī. <i>Viparītāni</i>, “contrary” or “reversed”: the omens point the wrong way. <i>Na ca śreyo’nupaśyāmi</i>, “I do not see the good”: Arjuna has taken his own sight as the final word. The Gītā will soon distinguish <i>śreyas</i>, the truly good, from <i>preyas</i>, the pleasant (an idea from the Kaṭha Upaniṣad 1.2.1–2). In 3.35 and 18.47 Kṛṣṇa will say what is truly good for Arjuna: his own dharma, done without selfish motive.',
 'When we feel strongly, we see signs that agree with us. Wise people treat their own certainty as a hypothesis, not a fact.')
DEEP[32]=('Arjuna calls Kṛṣṇa <i>Govinda</i>, “finder or protector of cows”, a name from his childhood among the cowherds of Vraja. It is a tender, personal name.',
 'This verse sounds like the ideal of <i>vairāgya</i>, dispassion, that the Gītā itself praises (6.35). Commentators ask why Kṛṣṇa does not accept it. Śaṅkara and others answer: true dispassion is born of discrimination (<i>viveka</i>), seeing what is lasting and what is not. Arjuna’s comes from grief. Kṛṣṇa’s later teaching (2.47, 3.19) is not to give up action but to give up the craving for its fruits. Arjuna here wants to give up the action itself because the fruit has become painful. Same words, opposite spirit.',
 'Dispassion that comes from understanding brings peace. Dispassion that comes from disappointment usually brings more pain. It is worth knowing which one we feel.')
DEEP[33]=('The Pāṇḍavas’ claim was to their father’s share of the kingdom. Kingdoms were held for the family, to support kin, priests, servants and subjects.',
 '<i>Prāṇāṃs tyaktvā dhanāni ca</i>, “giving up their lives and wealth”: Arjuna sees the people he would share his kingdom with ready to lose everything. Commentators find his reasoning sound as far as it goes: wealth without those it was meant for is empty. But Kṛṣṇa will widen the frame. The question is not “what do I get?” but “what is my duty, and who am I?”. In 2.31–33 he will speak of a warrior’s duty to resist injustice; in 2.12–13, of the Self beyond death. Both move the discussion away from gain and loss.',
 'When a goal no longer makes sense, ask what it was for. Sometimes the purpose remains even when the plan must change.')
DEEP[34]=('Among those Arjuna lists: <i>ācāryāḥ</i>, teachers, Droṇa and Kṛpa; <i>pitāmahāḥ</i>, grandfathers, Bhīṣma; <i>mātulāḥ</i>, uncles, Śalya; <i>śvaśurāḥ</i>, fathers-in-law; <i>pautrāḥ</i>, grandsons; <i>śyālāḥ</i>, brothers-in-law.',
 'The list in 1.34 nearly repeats 1.26. Sañjaya first reported what Arjuna saw; now Arjuna himself says it aloud. Commentators observe that speaking it changes it: seeing was shock, saying is grief. Kṛṣṇa remains silent throughout Arjuna’s speech (1.28–46). Many teachers hold this up as a model: Kṛṣṇa lets the student pour out everything before he says a word. The teaching that follows in chapter 2 is powerful partly because it was preceded by patient listening.',
 'Listening fully before answering is a gift. Kṛṣṇa’s silence in chapter 1 is as much part of his teaching as his words in chapter 2.')
DEEP[35]=('<i>Trailokya-rājya</i>, rule over the three worlds: heaven, earth and the lower regions. In the Purāṇas, such rule was the prize of great kings and gods; it is the biggest prize Arjuna can imagine.',
 '<i>Ghnato’pi</i>, “even if they kill me”: Arjuna offers his own life rather than harm others. Some readers hear in this verse an early echo of non-violence; others, the despair of a man who would rather die than face a terrible choice. The name <i>Madhusūdana</i> recalls Viṣṇu’s killing of the demons Madhu and Kaiṭabha, a story told in the Devī Māhātmya and the Purāṇas. Commentators read the name as Arjuna’s silent plea: “you destroy demons, but these are my elders”. Kṛṣṇa’s reply, beginning in 2.11, does not deny their goodness but changes how Arjuna sees death itself.',
 'Being willing to suffer rather than harm is a mark of a good heart. The harder question, which the Gītā asks, is what a good heart should do when others will suffer if it does nothing.')
DEEP[36]=('<i>Ātatāyin</i>, aggressor: law-books such as the Manusmṛti (8.350–351) and Vasiṣṭha Dharmasūtra list six kinds, including arsonists, poisoners, armed attackers, robbers of land and abductors of wives. The Kauravas had done several: the lac house, the poison given to Bhīma, the seizure of the kingdom, Draupadī’s humiliation.',
 '<i>Janārdana</i>, “the one who moves people” or “destroyer of wrongdoers”, is the name Arjuna uses here, perhaps hinting: you destroy wrongdoers, but I cannot. The heart of the verse is <i>pāpam evāśrayed asmān</i>, “only sin would cling to us”. Arjuna feels a moral weight that the law-book does not remove. Commentators find this a real dilemma of dharmas: law permits, conscience recoils. Kṛṣṇa’s answer in 2.38 and 5.10 is that action done without selfish motive, with equanimity, does not bind with sin, like a lotus leaf untouched by water.',
 'When law and conscience disagree, neither should simply win. Look for the deeper principle that respects what each is trying to protect.')
DEEP[37]=('The word <i>sva</i>, own, appears again and again in his speech.',
 'This verse is the first time Arjuna draws a conclusion: <i>tasmān nārhā vayaṃ hantum</i>, “therefore we should not kill”. Kṛṣṇa will answer with his own conclusions, each beginning with <i>tasmāt</i>, “therefore”: <i>tasmād yudhyasva bhārata</i>, “therefore fight” (2.18), and <i>tasmād uttiṣṭha</i>, “therefore stand up” (2.37). The Gītā is in part a debate of “therefores”. Commentators note how the word <i>sva</i>, one’s own, changes meaning across the Gītā: here it means kin; in 3.35 <i>svadharma</i> means one’s own duty; in 6.5 <i>ātmanā ātmānam</i> means oneself by oneself. The Gītā moves “own” from family to duty to Self.',
 'Who we call “our own” shapes everything we do. Try widening the circle, and watch how your choices change.')
DEEP[38]=('Arjuna sees Duryodhana’s side as driven by greed for the kingdom. Greed, <i>lobha</i>, is named in 16.21 as one of the three gates of hell, with desire and anger.',
 '<i>Mitra-drohe ca pātakam</i>, “and the sin of betraying friends”: the Kauravas betrayed friendship many times, from the lac house to the dice game. Commentators praise Arjuna’s refusal to use the other side’s wrong as an excuse for his own. The Gītā later describes the ideal person as <i>adveṣṭā</i>, without hatred (12.13), and <i>samaḥ śatrau ca mitre ca</i>, the same to friend and foe (12.18). Arjuna’s instinct here points in that direction. His conclusion will still change, but his refusal to hate remains.',
 'Other people’s wrong does not excuse ours. Keep your own standard high even when others drop theirs.')
DEEP[39]=('Arjuna uses the word <i>prapaśyadbhiḥ</i>, “by us who clearly see”, claiming clear sight.',
 'The argument runs: they are blinded by greed, we are not; so we should turn back. It is a reasoned case, and many readers find it moving. Commentators note its flaw: Arjuna treats the family as the highest good. The Gītā does not disregard the family, but it sets it within a wider dharma: protecting the world from injustice (<i>loka-saṅgraha</i>, 3.20, 3.25). It also sets it within the knowledge of the Self, which does not die when the body dies (2.20). Arjuna is reasoning well inside a frame that is too small.',
 'Sound reasoning inside the wrong frame can still lead to the wrong answer. Sometimes the question is not “is my logic right?” but “is my frame big enough?”')
DEEP[40]=('<i>Kula-dharmāḥ sanātanāḥ</i>, “the age-old family duties”: these included household rites, the sacred fire, offerings to ancestors, rules of marriage and inheritance, and the passing on of knowledge from elder to younger.',
 'The word <i>adharma</i>, lawlessness, appears for the first time here. Kṛṣṇa will use the same word in his famous promise: <i>yadā yadā hi dharmasya glānir bhavati bhārata, abhyutthānam adharmasya</i>, “whenever dharma declines and adharma rises, I come” (4.7). Arjuna fears that the war will cause adharma; Kṛṣṇa will reveal that adharma is already ruling, and the war is how dharma is restored. The same word sits on both sides of the argument. Commentators admire Arjuna’s concern for continuity; the Gītā’s answer is that dharma’s survival depends on more than one family’s survival.',
 'Ask what really carries a tradition forward. Sometimes it is people and rituals; sometimes it is courage to stand for what the tradition was meant to protect.')
DEEP[41]=('<i>Vārṣṇeya</i>, “descendant of Vṛṣṇi”, is Kṛṣṇa’s clan name. <i>Varṇa-saṅkara</i>, “mixing of orders”, was a central fear of the law-books, which saw social order as tied to lineage and duty.',
 'This verse has drawn much modern discussion. In its ancient setting, it voices a fear that the loss of men in war would leave women unprotected and families broken; the Mahābhārata’s Strī Parva, the book of the women, shows the grief of widows after the war. Some modern commentators read the verse historically; others read it as Arjuna’s argument that the Gītā goes on to transcend. Kṛṣṇa himself later says he created the four orders by guṇa and karma, quality and action (4.13), shifting the basis from birth to nature. The Gītā does not settle every social question, and readers continue to differ.',
 'Old texts carry the assumptions of their times. Reading them well means seeing both the lasting concern and the time-bound form.')
DEEP[42]=('<i>Piṇḍa</i>, rice-balls, and <i>udaka</i>, water, are offered to ancestors in the <i>śrāddha</i> rites. These are still performed by many Hindu families, especially in the fortnight of Pitṛ Pakṣa.',
 '<i>Patanti pitaro hy eṣām</i>, “their ancestors fall”: in the old belief, ancestors depend on offerings from their descendants. Arjuna’s fear reaches back through the generations. The Gītā does not dismiss ancestor rites; Kṛṣṇa mentions worship of ancestors in 9.25. But he will teach that the Self is unborn and eternal (2.20), and that each being reaches the destination it has prepared through its own action and devotion (8.6, 9.25). The deepest offering, he will say, is a life dedicated to the Lord (9.27).',
 'Honouring those who came before us is a sacred act. Living a good life may be the best offering we can give them.')
DEEP[43]=('<i>Jāti-dharmāḥ</i>, duties of community, and <i>kula-dharmāḥ</i>, duties of the family: Arjuna names two levels of tradition. Both were thought to be <i>śāśvata</i>, everlasting.',
 'Arjuna pictures a whole social world collapsing. Commentators point to a quiet turn in the Gītā’s vocabulary: what Arjuna calls <i>śāśvata</i> here, Kṛṣṇa will use of the Self (2.20, <i>śāśvato’yaṃ purāṇaḥ</i>) and of the Lord’s abode (18.56). He does not say family duties are worthless; he points to what is truly everlasting. The tradition has always held these two together: respect for custom, and the knowledge that the Self is beyond all custom.',
 'Ask of any custom: what is its purpose? If the purpose is good, honour it. If the form has drifted from the purpose, the purpose matters more.')
DEEP[44]=('<i>Iti anuśuśruma</i>, “so we have heard”: Arjuna appeals to <i>śruti</i> and <i>smṛti</i>, sacred tradition as handed down orally. In ancient India, learning was by hearing from a teacher.',
 'Commentators find this honesty touching: Arjuna does not claim to know; he reports what he has heard. Later Kṛṣṇa will say <i>tad viddhi praṇipātena paripraśnena sevayā</i>: “learn it by bowing, by questioning, by serving” those who have seen the truth (4.34). Hearing is the first step; questioning and seeing follow. The Upaniṣads give the same order: <i>śravaṇa</i>, hearing; <i>manana</i>, reflecting; <i>nididhyāsana</i>, contemplation (Bṛhadāraṇyaka 2.4.5). Arjuna is at step one. The Gītā will take him to the end.',
 'What we were taught is a gift. Taking the next step, asking and reflecting, turns that gift into our own understanding.')
DEEP[45]=('Before the war, the Pāṇḍavas, through Kṛṣṇa as envoy, asked for just five villages. Duryodhana refused even a needle-point of land. Arjuna’s charge of greed against his own side is not fair, and grief has blurred his memory.',
 '<i>Aho bata</i>, “alas, indeed”: an exclamation of deep regret. Commentators see Arjuna turning the blame on himself, as grief often does. The Gītā will later name such self-condemnation as a kind of confusion too. In 4.36 Kṛṣṇa says that even the worst wrongdoer can cross all sin on the boat of knowledge; in 18.66 he says, “do not grieve”. Guilt that leads to correction is healthy. Guilt that paralyses is another form of <i>moha</i>, delusion.',
 'In grief we often judge ourselves harshly and unfairly. Pause before accepting the verdict of a hurting mind.')
DEEP[46]=('In the code of war, killing an unarmed, non-resisting man was a grave wrong. Arjuna would put the sin on the Kauravas rather than himself.',
 'Commentators debate the verse. Some see a sincere preference for suffering over harming. Others note the hidden calculation: by dying unarmed, Arjuna would escape responsibility, leaving his brothers and allies to face the war without him. Gandhi’s Anāsakti Yoga reads the war as a picture of the inner struggle and draws non-violence from the Gītā’s teaching of selfless action. Tilak, in Gītā Rahasya, reads Kṛṣṇa’s answer as a call to active duty. Both are respected readings.',
 'Ask whether stepping back protects others or only protects us from a hard choice. Sometimes the brave thing is to stay and act well.')
DEEP[47]=('Arjuna sits on the <i>ratha-upastha</i>, the chariot seat or floor. In the epic, he now sits between two armies, while thousands wait.',
 'The chapter ends with the colophon: <i>arjuna-viṣāda-yogo nāma prathamo’dhyāyaḥ</i>. Every chapter of the Gītā carries such a colophon calling the text an Upaniṣad, a science of Brahman and a scripture of yoga, in the form of a dialogue between Śrī Kṛṣṇa and Arjuna. Commentators say even despair is a yoga when it turns us towards the teacher. Madhusūdana calls chapter 1 the necessary ground of the whole teaching. Śaṅkara’s commentary treats <i>śoka</i> and <i>moha</i>, grief and delusion, as the disease the Gītā cures. Chapter 2 opens with Arjuna in tears.',
 'If you are at your lowest, you are not lost; you are at the start. Arjuna’s collapse became the occasion for the most famous teaching in India.')
